{"k": "general:0e06664ee7bd6070f7c9efaca2aaa0276aac45ff16f4e06edd984b1f6f512268", "v": {"text": "To find the **speed** at which the stone hits the ground, we can use the **kinematic equation** for motion under constant acceleration:\n\n$$\nv^2 = u^2 + 2gh\n$$\n\nWhere:\n\n- $ v $ = final velocity (what we're solving for)\n- $ u $ = initial velocity = 0 m/s (since the stone is dropped)\n- $ g $ = acceleration due to gravity = 9.81 m/s\u00b2\n- $ h $ = height = 45 m\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Plug in the values\n\n$$\nv^2 = 0^2 + 2 \\times 9.81 \\times 45\n$$\n\n$$\nv^2 = 2 \\times 9.81 \\times 45 = 882.9\n$$\n\n$$\nv = \\sqrt{882.9} \\approx 29.71 \\, \\text{m/s}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{29.71 \\text{ m/s}}\n$$\n\nThe stone hits the ground at approximately **29.71 m/s**.", "tokens": 254}}
