{"k": "general:d6ba9196ad66280cc1bc334ae9aedb122ee78e6ae834de8131a38631a0545a0e", "v": {"text": "To find out how much **energy** is required to raise the temperature of the water, we can use the specific heat formula:\n\n$$\nQ = m \\cdot c \\cdot \\Delta T\n$$\n\nWhere:\n\n- $ Q $ is the energy in joules (J)\n- $ m = 50 \\, \\text{kg} $ is the mass of the water\n- $ c = 4186 \\, \\text{J/(kg\u00b7K)} $ is the specific heat of water\n- $ \\Delta T = 30 \\, \\text{K} $ is the temperature change\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the energy in joules\n\n$$\nQ = 50 \\cdot 4186 \\cdot 30\n$$\n\n$$\nQ = 50 \\cdot 125,580 = 6,279,000 \\, \\text{J}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Convert to megajoules (MJ)\n\n$$\n1 \\, \\text{MJ} = 1,000,000 \\, \\text{J}\n$$\n\n$$\nQ = \\frac{6,279,000}{1,000,000} = 6.279 \\, \\text{MJ}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{6.28 \\, \\text{MJ}}\n$$", "tokens": 314}}
