{"k": "general:929592cd7428ac5b2c638f55107601a7854b3752a5af21a94a9ad997fe5f9675", "v": {"text": "To find the **circular orbital speed** at an altitude of **400 km** above Earth, we can use the formula for orbital speed:\n\n$$\nv = \\sqrt{\\frac{GM}{r}}\n$$\n\nWhere:\n- $ GM = 3.986 \\times 10^{14} \\, \\text{m}^3/\\text{s}^2 $ (standard gravitational parameter of Earth)\n- $ r $ is the distance from the center of the Earth to the satellite, which is:\n\n$$\nr = R_{\\text{Earth}} + \\text{altitude} = 6371 \\, \\text{km} + 400 \\, \\text{km} = 6771 \\, \\text{km} = 6,771,000 \\, \\text{m}\n$$\n\nNow plug the values into the formula:\n\n$$\nv = \\sqrt{\\frac{3.986 \\times 10^{14}}{6,771,000}} = \\sqrt{5.893 \\times 10^6} \\approx 2427.5 \\, \\text{m/s}\n$$\n\nConvert to **km/s**:\n\n$$\nv \\approx 2.4275 \\, \\text{km/s}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 Final Answer:\n$$\n\\boxed{2.43 \\, \\text{km/s}}\n$$", "tokens": 318}}
